// HumanityOS ship structure: the mothership's SHARED spaces and where homes go
// (docs/design/ship-homes-and-logistics.md, increment 1a; the zone model is increments A
// and B of docs/design/ship-superstructure.md). One frame for the whole ship: metres, y up,
// the origin at plot p1's corner.
//
//   zones      pressurized shared spaces: each an id, label, purpose
//              (commons|street|bay|agriculture|corridor|residence), a ship `origin` for its
//              box min corner (y = deck height), and a body in zone-local metres. Homes are
//              NOT zones here: each player's home is a design file (data/homes/<kind>.ron)
//              that the loader places at that player's plot as zone "home".
//   corridors  straight walk-through tubes between two zones. Each row OWNS its door mouths:
//              lat = the tube centreline's SHIP coordinate on the axis across the run (z for
//              an X run, x for a Z run); door_width/door_height = the aperture it cuts through
//              each zone's shell. Never author a wall flush with a corridor mouth (two
//              coplanar walls z-fight and one of them has no collision).
//   plots      where homes go: id, kind (which home designs fit), origin + size (w, h, d),
//              and the door: the zone its corridor runs to plus that corridor's own lat,
//              width and mouth. A plot's door lat must meet its design's door point.
//   default_plot  the plot offline play uses.
//   districts  the mothership's macro districts (residential, hangar, the Concourse, ...),
//              labelled volumes in ship metres drawn with their zone_filler.ron contents.
//              Residential districts draw nothing: their homes are the plots, each drawn as
//              its holder's own home or, since increment 2, a neighbour's default design.
//   id         the ship's name for a relay: the world its plots are held on (increment 1b,
//              src/relay/storage/plots.rs). Changing it starts every plot afresh on a relay.
//
// validate() refuses overlapping plots, zones and corridor tubes (touching is fine).
// Districts are areas, not boxes, and are not checked.
//
// TWELVE HOMES ALONG FIRST STREET (2026-10-04, "More plots along First Street, as built" in the
// design doc). p1 and p2 stand where increment 1a put them, and never move: a relay's stored
// claims (`game_plots`) and a player's remembered plot name plots by id. p3 to p12 continue down
// the WEST side of First Street, each 55 x 89 m and 99 m on from the last (a 10 m gap, as between
// p1 and p2), each with its door corridor to the street at the home's door, local z 40 (so lat =
// origin z + 40). Only the west side: the homestead's door is in its east wall and a plot cannot
// turn yet, so a home east of the street would face away from it, which `assemble` refuses. First
// Street runs z 85 to 1185, 7 m past p12's end; the residential district holds the plots, their
// corridors, the street and the Commons; the Concourse and the Transit Hub, which stood just past
// the street's old end at z 205, moved 20 m east, off it. The ship is 1.2 km long now, past the
// relay's 250 m view (data/ship/shared_world.ron): neighbours see each other, and the far end of
// the street is out of view of the Commons. A new plot added here (the Dev Plots panel's "Add
// plot" copies the last one, past it) needs First Street to reach past its door. First Street has
// no lights of its own, as before: it is lit through its glass roof, and every zone's lights are
// charged to each player's home meter today (lib.rs, the house lighting consumer).
//
// THE CABIN BLOCK (ship homes increment 6, docs/design/ship-homes-increment-6-plan.md section
// 3.3). East of First Street's north end, a block of sixteen cabins on one hall:
//   - the hall, zone `cabins-1` "Cabin Block A" (residence), x 77..80, z 88..136, 3 m high under
//     a glass roof and with no lights of its own (like First Street: every zone's lights are
//     charged to each player's own meter today). The zone's own floor IS the hall (local z
//     0..40), and its one volume is the block's shared WASHROOM, `washroom-1` (local z 40..48, a
//     room_bathroom), behind an interior wall with a door. The hall is not a volume of its own,
//     though the plan drew one: the relay makes every zone and every volume a room and its
//     explore quest asks a player to visit each, and a zone wholly divided into volumes has no
//     floor of its own to visit (the relay test `every_room_of_the_ship_has_a_floor_of_its_own`).
//     The washroom's fixtures wait for ship machines that leave the households' meters (plan 3.8);
//   - its corridor from First Street: an X run across x 75..77 at z 92, a 2 m door;
//   - sixteen CABIN PLOTS, c1 to c16, kind "cabin", each 3.15 x 3.0 x 2.5 m at x 80.5..83.65 and
//     z 88 + 2.5 (i - 1): the shipped cabin (data/homes/shipped/cabin.ron), whose door is in its
//     west wall's middle, so each plot's door corridor runs 0.5 m west into the hall at z 89.25 +
//     2.5 (i - 1), a 0.9 x 2.0 m door in a 1.2 m tube. Neighbouring cabins share a wall face,
//     which validate() allows; their tubes are 2.5 m apart and 1.2 m wide, so none overlaps. The
//     cabins are 3.15 m wide so each room is England's single bedroom INSIDE its walls (7.54 m2;
//     the design file says why). East of them, nothing stands until the districts at x 200.
//   - A block is this ZONE, not a parent plot: a walkable shared hall cannot sit inside a plot
//     (validate() refuses a zone overlapping a plot), and a parent plot's own door tube would
//     overlap its cabins' tubes. A cabin's block is the zone its door opens on.
// THE RECREATION ROOM: the Commons' south-west corner, the volume `recreation` (Commons-local
// x 0.5..10.5, z 43.5..51.5), 3.5 m from the corridor to First Street, 12 m from where a guest
// arrives and clear of every crew walk; its couches, rug and screen are data/machines/ship.ron's.
// THE SOUTH COMMONS: zone `commons-2`, a second Commons halfway down First Street (x 77..101,
// z 770..810, glass roof, no lights), after the Commons in this list so a guest still arrives
// in the first; its mess hall, `mess-hall-2`, is its north strip with a serving counter and a
// food store of its own (data/food/ship_stores.ron), reached by a corridor from the street at
// z 790. It is there for the design's rule that every home is within five minutes' walk of a
// mess hall (docs/design/ship-homes-and-logistics.md section 2.9; the test
// `every_home_reaches_a_mess_hall_within_five_minutes` in src/ship/ship_structure.rs): at 1.4 m/s,
// p5 to p12 are 5.0 to 13.3 minutes from the Commons' mess hall along the street, and with the
// South Commons every home is within 4.6 minutes (p5) of one of the two.
//
// The relay's world is this file (increment 3, src/relay/handlers/ship_world.rs): every zone is
// a room of it, and so is every labelled volume in a zone's body (the Commons' "mess-hall", the
// north strip along its first wall, where the crew eat and the ship's food stores stand,
// data/food/ship_stores.ron). The crew's chore spots (data/npc/chores.ron) are in those rooms'
// own metres, so moving the mess hall or the Commons' room block means checking them (the relay
// test `every_crew_walk_between_its_sites_is_clear_of_walls` says which one a wall now crosses).
//
// The Commons' `spawn` is where a guest arrives (a player with no plot of their own, and a
// scripted player or AI agent that names no ship; `ShipStructure::guest_spawn`): the open floor
// south-east of its room block, (87.5, 67.5) on the ship, at least 7 m from every wall. Until
// increment 4 it had none and a guest arrived in the Commons' middle, (82, 47.5), which is inside
// the walled room block; a walk out of there in a straight line met a wall, and with the relay's
// speed check nobody can be teleported out any more.
//
// Materials: 0 = grid, 1 = steel/metal, 2 = concrete, 3 = wood, 4 = glass.
//
// Editor saves rewrite everything BELOW this header, and only in the Dev play mode: body
// comments are dropped, floats are re-printed (0.70 -> 0.7), and unknown fields are dropped.
// Durable notes belong here or in the design docs, not beside the records.

